Certainly you're right, that charging would be the easier way for that.
In my games, though, I've observed that random charging scrolls are not
too common, and (call it stinginess or ressource management) I spend or
save them (in case I have one) for something essential - that is, as long
as it is appropriate to do so.

>> [...], but it makes no sense,
>> IMO, if you program your game to deliver wands of charge=0 regularily.
> [...]
> The rationalization behind that is [...]
> who would let a /oW lying around without using it?

Not sure what you're aiming at.

Random WoW's are rare. If I'd find one before the castle I'd either save
it [very early game] until I know what will best increase my chances to
win, or apply it [mid game] when it's clear what I am severely missing,
or, as a corner case (in my games), to solve some concrete problem I am
facing where there's no workaround available. The point between early/mid
game is often determined by the Sokoban price but of course also depending
to a huge degree what a game has already provided at that point.

